H2: Known Faults - Volvo XC40
P: The faults below come from What Car?'s reliability pages for the XC40 (2018-present) and the XC40 Recharge (2021-present), plus one Top Gear long-term test.
H3: Infotainment and Driver Aid Glitches
P: MONITOR ú 2018 on ú Get a quote
P: What Car? says there have been minor electronic glitches on the XC40, particularly with the infotainment system and electronic driver aids. The same page says owners mentioned only a few significant mechanical issues. If the screen or a driver aid misbehaves on a test drive, get a quote before you agree a price.
H3: Recharge Warning Lights and Software Faults
P: MONITOR ú Recharge (2021 on) ú Get a quote
P: For the electric Recharge, What Car? says several owners reported persistent issues with warning lights and software faults, particularly affecting the infotainment and battery management systems. On a test drive, watch the dashboard and run through the infotainment menus.
H3: Recharge Dealer Visits
P: CHECK ú Recharge (2021 on) ú Ask the seller
P: What Car? says that in more than one case, these Recharge problems required multiple dealer visits to resolve. It sums up the experience as mixed results for reliability and dealer service. Top Gear's long-term Recharge had a series of niggles, nothing terminal, in its words, which led the magazine to alert Volvo. That is one car, but it is worth asking any seller whether the car has needed repeat visits.
H3: Electric Tailgate
P: CHECK ú Electric tailgate ú Test it
P: What Car? says to check that the tailgate operates correctly, if it's electric. Open and close it several times from the key and from the button before you buy.
H3: Alloy Wheel Kerb Damage
P: MINOR ú Any miles ú Inspect the wheels
P: What Car? also advises checking the alloy wheels for any kerb damage. Look at every wheel in daylight and mention any marks when you talk price.
H3: DVSA Safety Recalls
P: DVSA's recall data lists several XC40 recalls, including these.
LI: On affected vehicles the High Voltage (HV) Battery may have a manufacturing defect which could cause the Battery Cells to overheat when the HV Battery is fully charged.
LI: Affected vehicles may experience a temporary loss of braking functionality after coasting downhill with 'B' mode selected or when using One Pedal Drive.
LI: Due to a software error the left rear indicator may stop working.
LI: During driving or idle the O-Ring may become twisted or break.
LI: There may be a potential software error in the brake control module.
P: Enter the registration in our recall check to see whether a car has a recall that is still open.
