H2: Known Faults - Volvo XC90
P: The faults below are split by generation. The first group is for the 2015-on car, the second for the 2002-2014 car.
H3: Electrical Faults and Sensors
P: MONITOR ú 2015 on ú Get a quote
P: What Car? says that while many owners praise both petrol and diesel models for being generally robust, a noticeable trend of electrical faults and costly sensor issues emerged. Carbuyer says that beyond the recalls, the XC90 Mk2 suffers from assorted electrical issues, with some owners reporting multiple electrical failures.
H3: Diesel EGR and AdBlue
P: MONITOR ú 2015 on ú Get a quote
P: Autocar says that with diesels, it tends to see problems with the EGR or the AdBlue systems.
H3: Central Display
P: MONITOR ú 2015 on ú Get a quote
P: Autocar says the central display can become inoperative, meaning you can't use the sat-nav or climate controls.
H3: T8 Rear Electric Drive
P: HIGH ú T8, 2015 on ú Get a quote
P: What Car? says early cars can suffer ERAD problems, causing juddering, hesitation, loss of electric drive or unusual noises from the rear. Autocar says the electric rear drive axle is the weak part of the hybrid system, and that if it packs up Volvo can sometimes pay 50% of the bill as it is a œ4000 repair. Auto Express says that if a T8 hesitates as it is pulling away, the electric motor on the rear axle might be failing, and that repairs cost in excess of œ3,000.
H3: D5 Diesel Injectors
P: MONITOR ú 2002-2014 ú Get a quote
P: Carbuyer says that if the D5 diesel struggles to start in cold weather, it could need new injectors, costing a hefty œ1,500.
H3: Electrical Glitches
P: MINOR ú 2002-2014 ú Get a quote
P: Carbuyer says electrical glitches are quite common on the 2002-2014 car, but something as simple as tightening the battery connectors can sometimes cure them.
H3: Automatic Gearbox Slipping
P: CHECK ú 2002-2014 ú Get a quote
P: One owner's review of a 2006 car on Carbuyer says the car has a problem with the auto gearbox slipping.
H3: T8 Battery Warranty
P: CHECK ú T8, 2015 on ú Get a quote
P: Volvo Cars UK covers the high-voltage hybrid battery for eight years from the date the car is delivered to the first owner or 100,000 miles, whichever comes first. Volvo adds that loss of battery capacity due to or resulting from normal gradual capacity loss is not covered under the Volvo new car warranty.
H3: DVSA Safety Recalls
P: DVSA's recall data lists several XC90 recalls, including these.
LI: On affected vehicles the front left seat bolts may not have been torqued according to specification which could reduce the occupant protection in an impact.
LI: The anchorage bolts for the second-row seat belts and seat belt buckles may not have been correctly secured.
LI: The thrust bearing in the turbo charger may not be robust enough.
LI: There is a risk that the 15amp fuse could blow which will prevent the low pressure fuel pump operating.
P: Enter the registration in our recall check to see whether a car has a recall that is still open.
